From 8e269edf3f8ea44b51b2a4fe90e1445b04ebea2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 18:58:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard printf: C-exact numbers, long-double
 models, output cap, whole-argument walk kept

Review 5896107359 of PR #2506 (t_01a1d6250699b791) found that the printf model formatted numbers
with Python's `%` operator, whose corners differ from C. Under a sink, those digits can name the
script: `printf 'bash D/%#x.sh' 0 | bash` runs D/0.sh, but the model printed D/0x0.sh, so the
guard scanned the wrong file.

- New cron/lifecycle_guard_printf_number.py: C integer formatting written out by hand
  (strtoimax/strtoumax clamps and wrap, `#` on 0, `0` ignored with a precision, `%+.0d` of 0).
  Floats are rounded to a long double model and printed exactly with Fraction. The models are
  x86 80-bit, IEEE double (Apple Silicon) and IEEE quad (Linux arm64), and the walk prints
  floats under every model, so the host's is always walked.
- printf_output stops once its output passes PRINTF_MAX_OUTPUT (1 MiB, pinned equal to the
  guard's scan budget), so a 30 KB `%4096s` format no longer builds 20 MB.
- printed_argument_lines still yields the old whole-argument substitution next to the model,
  so the model can only add text to scan (fail closed).

Proof: a differential fuzz against bash 5 printf (LC_ALL=C, x86-64) gives 0 mismatches in about
40k cases across diouxX, eEfFgG and aA. The DOUBLE model matches Python's C-double `%` formatting
and float.hex in 25k cases. New byte-exact bash rows and _NUMBER_SINK_SHAPES were added. At
4b6af22a1f, 6 of the 7 sink shapes are red (alt-hex-zero, alt-octal-zero-precision,
plus-zero-precision, long-double-f, x87-hex-float, double-hex-float); double-f already passed
there, because Python formats a double. All are green here. Mutants: 21/21 killed.
heredoc_shape_corpus report --full:
cases=22212 violations=0. tests/cron plus guard importers: 3094 passed, 0 failed.
